almost 1/5 of the world's population has  no access to electricity so have to use  other means to heat and light their  homes which are often expensive  dangerous and unhealthy lamps filled  with kerosene are the main source of  light in households in the developing  world  but living with one of these over the  course of the day is the equivalent to  smoking 40 cigarettes Jim Reeves and his  team think they have a solution they've  designed a light that is powered by  gravity and has no running costs we were  presented with the challenge of trying  to developer a viable alternative to  kerosene lanterns for our Solar charity  and we came up with a solution of  gravity light which uses a lifted weight  in the same way sort of principle has  been used for the generations to drive  things like their grandfather clocks  rather than the two or three minutes it  takes to charge a wind-up light to  charge gravity light it takes just three  seconds for 30 minutes of light the user  lifts the 10 kilogram bag then a series  of gears turn powering a low-cost  generator which turns the light on no  fuel is needed just the effort of  lifting the bag it's hoped it will sell  for under $10 it's been four and a half  years in the making and gone through  lots of iterations but a thousand units  are now being trialed in 26 countries  purpose of the trial is partly to  understand  NEADS as if as a concept but also to  understand what kind of rigors and  challenges that the system is going to  have to withstand one man who's managed  to make a successful international  business already is the founder of  children's case company trunky I asked  him for his take on the light I think  it's very interesting concept I think  the real key for all physical product  that is going to sell it and do well in  developing markets is that they're able  to be fixed by the local people so if it  breaks it can be serviced without  overseas components we're very mindful  of the fact that for the device to be  really as long-lived as it could be and  the idea of having serviceable parts is  almost essential we're looking in the in  the medium term at either starting with  some in-country assembly ultimately  perhaps in country manufacturer gravity  light has the potential to lift millions  out of poverty but the next challenge is  bringing it to scale to make it  affordable for all
 

